# AItelier environment variables — example
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in your keys, then load them into your shell:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   source .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored; this `.env.example` is safe to commit (no secrets).
# Provider base URLs are configured in llm_providers.json — only the keys live here.

# AItelier is PROVIDER-AGNOSTIC, in TWO layers:
#
#   llm_providers.json   provider name -> base_url + the NAME of the key it reads
#   model_routes.json    INTERNAL model name -> ordered provider/model candidates
#
# Both are GITIGNORED like this file, and ship as .example — which vendors you
# hold accounts with is yours, not ours:
#   cp llm_providers.example.json llm_providers.json
#   cp model_routes.example.json  model_routes.json
# The internal NAMES (flash / pro / glm / smart / vision) are the contract that
# agent_configs reference; the endpoints behind them are your choice. Missing
# files fall back to the examples, so a fresh clone still starts.
#
# An agent_config's `model` names an internal model ("flash", "pro", "glm"), and
# model_routes.json says which real endpoints can serve it. The gateway binds
# the FIRST candidate and moves to the next only on an ENDPOINT error — a dead
# key, a spent token plan, a 429, a 5xx, a dropped connection. A bad request or
# an oversized context is never failed over: every candidate would reject it,
# and replaying it just burns the quota you are trying to conserve.
#
# A spent usage window parks that ONE endpoint until the provider's stated reset
# instant, so later steps skip it rather than re-paying the same doomed call;
# burst throttling does not park anything. Each turn's traced usage row records
# `served_by`, so you can tell afterwards which plan a run actually spent.
#
# It is STICKY, not round-robin. One gateway serves one pipeline step, so all of
# that step's turns hit one endpoint. Provider prefix caches are per-provider and
# this workload measures 26:1 prefill:decode at an 89.4% cache-hit rate;
# alternating endpoints per call turns cached input into full-price input and
# costs far more than a second token plan saves. Spread quota per RUN, not per
# call.
#
# So a second provider is worth adding even when you do not need its capacity:
# it is what turns "the plan ran out, everything stops until it resets" into
# "the next call goes elsewhere". Adding one is one entry in llm_providers.json,
# one candidate in model_routes.json, and a secret file — no code names a vendor.
# The shipped entries (deepseek, ark, localqwen) are examples; below are the
# keys THEY happen to read. See docs/external-dependencies.md.
#
# DOCKER: do NOT put this key here. It is delivered as a Docker secret (a file)
# so test/build subprocesses that inherit the environment never receive it:
#   mkdir -p ~/.aitelier-secrets && chmod 700 ~/.aitelier-secrets
#   printf '%s' "sk-..." > ~/.aitelier-secrets/DEEPSEEK_API_KEY && chmod 600 "$_"

# Volcengine Ark (token plan) — ONE key serves deepseek, glm, doubao, kimi and
# minimax through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint; llm_providers.json's `ark` entry
# is where a model gets registered. Same file-secret rule as the key above: in
# Docker it is a MOUNTED SECRET, not an env var, so build/test subprocesses that
# inherit os.environ never receive it.
#   printf '%s' "$ARK_KEY" > ~/.aitelier-secrets/ARK_API_KEY && chmod 600 "$_"
# The default roster runs on ark FIRST and falls over to deepseek direct, so
# ARK_API_KEY is REQUIRED and DEEPSEEK_API_KEY is RECOMMENDED — without the
# second one a spent Ark plan parks the scheduler until the window reopens
# (core/scheduler.py), instead of the run continuing elsewhere. Neither is
# hard-coded: `python -c "from core.external_deps import required_llm_keys,
# failover_llm_keys; print(required_llm_keys(), failover_llm_keys())"` derives
# both from the shipped agent_configs + model_routes.json.
# ai_router reads /run/secrets/DEEPSEEK_API_KEY. For LOCAL (non-docker) runs,
# either set AITELIER_SECRETS_DIR=~/.aitelier-secrets or export the key below.
# export DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=""

# GitHub auth for private-repo clone + push + PR (repository panel / clone mode).
# Same secret-file model — a FINE-GRAINED PAT, NOT in this file. Scope it to the
# repos you push to (Contents: R/W, Pull requests: R/W):
#   printf '%s' "github_pat_..." > ~/.aitelier-secrets/GITHUB_TOKEN && chmod 600 "$_"
# An empty/absent file = no credentials (public clone still works). The
# container's git-credential-helper.sh feeds it to github.com only.

# Optional: other providers are registered in llm_providers.json. Add a key here
# only if you point an agent_config at that provider.
#
# SEVERAL PLANS ON ONE VENDOR: a provider entry is a (base_url, key name) pair,
# not a vendor. Register it twice under different names with different
# api_key_env, list both as consecutive candidates in model_routes.json, and
# add the second key to docker-compose.yml's `secrets:` block. The spent-window
# cooldown keys on provider/model, so the two plans park independently.
# export MINIMAX_API_KEY=""

# Optional: model for skillflow "host"-delegated agents. The skill_converter
# pipeline (and any generated pipeline) declares its agents as model:"host" with
# the prompt embedded; AItelier maps that single token to one real model.
# Defaults to the INTERNAL name "flash", so host-delegated roles get the same
# failover as the named ones. A concrete provider/model still works.
# export AITELIER_HOST_AGENT_MODEL="flash"

# Optional: pin EVERY agent to one model, overriding the explicit per-role
# models in agent_configs/ (the production DPE mix puts architect / pm /
# final_verifier on a stronger model than the rest). For benchmark and ablation
# runs only: holding the model fixed is what makes a result attributable to the
# harness, and running with/without it measures the pro-vs-flash delta.
# Unset = no effect; the production mix stays the default.
# Applied BEFORE route resolution, so an internal name works here too.
# export AITELIER_FORCE_ALL_AGENT_MODELS="flash"

# Optional: web search backend.
# AItelier's web_search tool queries a SearXNG instance (JSON API). It is
# OPTIONAL — leave SEARXNG_URL unset and the pipeline runs fine without web
# search (agents fall back to model knowledge; web_fetch still works on any URL).
# To enable, point at your own SearXNG (recommended) or a public instance that
# exposes format=json:
# export SEARXNG_URL="http://localhost:8888"
# export SEARXNG_TIMEOUT="10"

# ── Cloudflare Access write-gate (Docker deployment) ──
# Two deployment shapes use the same three variables:
#
#   (a) Access in FRONT of the whole site — reads protected at the edge (any
#       logged-in user), writes restricted to the allowlist below.
#   (b) PUBLIC site, sign-in on one path — no Access application covers "/", so
#       reads are open to anyone; a second, path-scoped application protects
#       "/signin" alone. Visiting it authenticates the browser, Cloudflare sets
#       its cookie for the host, and /signin bounces to the app, which now reads
#       as a writer. AITELIER_SIGNIN_URL below is what puts the button in the UI.
#
# Either way the AUD must name an application that STILL EXISTS: delete the
# application and nothing can issue a credential this origin accepts, so every
# writer silently degrades to a reader as their cookie expires.
# Leave the pair blank to disable the gate entirely (local dev — all allowed).
# export AITELIER_CF_TEAM_DOMAIN="<team>.cloudflareaccess.com"
# export AITELIER_CF_AUD="<application-audience-aud-tag>"
# Comma-separated emails allowed to perform writes (create/modify projects).
# export AITELIER_WRITERS="you@example.com"
# Where the UI's "Sign in" button points. Leave unset and NO button is shown —
# a sign-in link with no application behind it is a link to nowhere.
# export AITELIER_SIGNIN_URL="https://your.host/signin"
# Shared secret the host CLI sends to bypass the write-gate (it has no JWT).
# Generate with:  python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
# export AITELIER_ADMIN_TOKEN="<random-secret>"

# ══ Things that are specific to ONE machine ═════════════════════════════════
# Everything below points at a host that is not part of this repo. All of it is
# optional; the defaults are the AUTHOR'S own boxes, so on any other machine you
# either point these somewhere real or leave the feature off. They are listed
# here because none of them announce themselves — a `docker compose up` succeeds
# and the gap only shows up when a run reaches the step that needs it.

# ── Publishing through an existing cloudflared connector ──
# Name the Docker network your cloudflared connector is on and the backend joins
# it, so the tunnel can reach `http://aitelier:4444`. Leave it unset and compose
# creates a throwaway network instead and AItelier stays on 127.0.0.1:4444.
#   docker network ls   → confirm the name on YOUR host, and that it is the one
#                         your connector is actually on: a name that matches no
#                         existing network is CREATED (empty), not refused.
# export AITELIER_EDGE_NETWORK="cloudflare_edge"
#
# This is ONE name in ONE file on purpose. It used to be an opt-in `-f
# docker-compose.edge.yml` overlay, and on 2026-08-25 a rebuild run as a plain
# `docker compose up -d` recreated the container without it: the container
# stayed healthy, 127.0.0.1:4444 kept answering 200, and the public path was
# gone with nothing saying so. Now there is no -f to forget.
#
# If the public path is dark while localhost answers, check the name first:
#   docker inspect aitelier --format '{{range $k,$v := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$k}} {{end}}'
# A typo'd name creates a new empty network rather than failing.

# ── Media generation (sprites, audio) ──
# The `gen_image_asset` / `gen_audio_asset` tools are thin clients over a shared
# MCP media server that keeps the models and the GPU on one box. Without it they
# raise MCPError naming the URL they could not reach; nothing else is affected.
# NOTE the MEDIA_ in the name: `AITELIER_MCP_URL` is the DSH plugin's variable
# for the opposite direction (where Harness finds AItelier).
# export AITELIER_MEDIA_MCP_URL="http://mcp_server:9003/mcp"
# export AITELIER_MEDIA_MCP_TIMEOUT="600"

# ── Godot readability gate (game projects only) ──
# `godot_vision` shows the play-test's rendered frames to a vision model and
# fails a game that is unreadable despite green assertions. Set in
# docker-compose.yml, where the default is a Tailscale address on the author's
# network. Point it at your own OpenAI-compatible vision endpoint, or accept
# that the gate reports itself BLIND rather than silently passing.
#   the judge is model_routes.json's `vision` route, NOT an env var;
#   GODOT_VISION_CONTEXT_TOKENS still sets the frame-batching budget and
#   GODOT_VISION_FALLBACK=0 pins it to the first judge
#
# The Godot COMPILE + play-test sidecar needs none of this — it ships in this
# repo as the `godot-builder` compose service.

# ── Self-hosted vLLM ──
# llm_providers.json's `localqwen` entry points at a Tailscale address on the
# author's network. Leave it alone unless you run your own vLLM: nothing selects
# that provider unless an agent_config names one of its models.
